 Yes, and if the plonkee is motivated to reform, the remaining members of
the newsgroup benefit even if the plonker were to leave the group
entirely. Publically announcing plonks also draws newbie's attention to
violations of a newsgroup's rules of courtesy so they may avoid such
behavior in the future. Not announcing plonks can lead to newbies saying
"Well, all these other people are doing it this way!" at which point you
have to explain to them that that part of the group has been silently
plonked as trolls/spammers/whatever by all the non-troll posters. That is
if the newbie doesn't get silently plonked by everybody without an
explanation either, which is even worse.

 When someone gets openly plonked it's tempting for them to assume that
it's just a case of neener-neener-neener and that it's all about
themselves, but the type of people who get plonked a lot are the sort who
tend to think the latter regardless of circumstance.

